WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.529 --> 00:00:08.529
Ahhh Germany.

00:00:08.529 --> 00:00:17.790
It’s the place where BMW, Ramstein, and
FKK (nudist) culture, and BMW cars were born.

00:00:17.790 --> 00:00:23.090
Nevertheless, Germany has something even more
important than all of that and that is that

00:00:23.090 --> 00:00:25.720
it is the world’s ultimate factory.

00:00:25.720 --> 00:00:29.980
Yep, you heard that right… and I know what
you are thinking…

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:34.640
“Isn’t China the country where TV screens,
mobile phones and, practically, every good

00:00:34.640 --> 00:00:37.500
we buy is made?”

00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:43.170
Well, that’s not entirely true.

00:00:43.170 --> 00:00:46.110
Now, of course, China does lead the charge
when it comes to exports.

00:00:46.110 --> 00:00:47.110
But…

00:00:47.110 --> 00:00:48.110
do you know what?

00:00:48.110 --> 00:00:53.269
In order to manufacture all those products,
they need to import a lot of things from abroad.

00:00:53.269 --> 00:00:56.409
Germany, instead, sells way more that what
it buys.

00:00:56.409 --> 00:01:01.329
In fact, 1 of every 10 products sold on the
international market is made in this country

00:01:01.329 --> 00:01:06.640
which has a population 15 times smaller than
China.

00:01:06.640 --> 00:01:12.719
Yes, we all know those big car companies:
BMW, Audi or VOLKSWAGEN…

00:01:12.719 --> 00:01:15.260
But there is way more than that!

00:01:15.260 --> 00:01:16.460
Don’t you believe me?

00:01:16.460 --> 00:01:17.880
Well, consider you mobile phone.

00:01:17.880 --> 00:01:22.759
Sure, I know it says right there ‘Assembled
in the People’s Republic of China’ or

00:01:22.759 --> 00:01:23.759
something similar.

00:01:23.759 --> 00:01:28.100
But, and I’m not recommending this, but
if you did open it up you are going to find

00:01:28.100 --> 00:01:30.329
a lot of microchips…

00:01:30.329 --> 00:01:33.659
As an example here , the little chip that
makes your screen rotate when you move the

00:01:33.659 --> 00:01:39.250
phone is made, most likely, by a German company
called BOSCH SENSORTECH.

00:01:39.250 --> 00:01:40.250
But not only that!

00:01:40.250 --> 00:01:44.810
Many of the robots used inside these Chinese
factories assembling the phones are probably

00:01:44.810 --> 00:01:46.979
made in Germany.

00:01:46.979 --> 00:01:56.000
In other words, in Germany, they manufacture
the things that go inside of the things manufactured

00:01:56.000 --> 00:01:58.109
by others.

00:01:58.109 --> 00:01:59.490
And now you might be wondering…

00:01:59.490 --> 00:02:01.659
How is that possible?

00:02:01.659 --> 00:02:04.359
Factories should be built in places with cheap
labour costs, right?

00:02:04.359 --> 00:02:08.780
I mean how is that possible that Germany,
with relatively high salaries, is such an

00:02:08.780 --> 00:02:10.130
industrial powerhouse?

00:02:10.130 --> 00:02:13.210
Well, today, we are going to answer that question.

00:02:13.210 --> 00:02:16.830
But before we do, let’s take a look back
at history...

00:02:16.830 --> 00:02:25.860
ORDOLIBERALISM

00:02:25.860 --> 00:02:28.860
After World War II, Europe was pretty darn
destroyed.

00:02:28.860 --> 00:02:32.830
Countries like Italy, France and Western Germany
(which was the capitalist side of Germany),

00:02:32.830 --> 00:02:39.280
got a massive money grant from their beloved
friend, Uncle Sam.

00:02:39.280 --> 00:02:44.110
But wait a minute because each of those countries
used that money in really different ways!

00:02:44.110 --> 00:02:47.610
The French president, for instance, was this
guy you see on the screen.

00:02:47.610 --> 00:02:51.100
General Charles de Gaulle.

00:02:51.100 --> 00:02:55.970
De Gaulle came from the military and was no
friend of private companies.

00:02:55.970 --> 00:03:01.110
His political creed, the so-called DIRIGISME,
was about nationalizing big businesses and

00:03:01.110 --> 00:03:07.260
leading the whole country’s economy as if
it was an army!

00:03:07.260 --> 00:03:10.819
In Western Germany they choose a different
path...

00:03:10.819 --> 00:03:15.879
Both the Chancellor KONRAD ADENAUER and his
finance minister, LUDWIG ERHARD, spent years

00:03:15.879 --> 00:03:18.070
thinking about their own economic system.

00:03:18.070 --> 00:03:22.849
A mixture between the competition of free
market capitalism and the safety net provided

00:03:22.849 --> 00:03:23.849
by the government.

00:03:23.849 --> 00:03:27.930
Then they decided to give this system the
worst name ever...

00:03:27.930 --> 00:03:31.810
They called it ORDOLIBERALISM or SOCIAL MARKET
ECONOMY.

00:03:31.810 --> 00:03:37.280
“Competition is the most promising means
to achieve and secure prosperity”—Ludwig

00:03:37.280 --> 00:03:38.280
Erhard

00:03:38.280 --> 00:03:39.569
In other words…

00:03:39.569 --> 00:03:43.860
While De Gaulle centralized the economy in
Paris, Konrad Adenauer designed a system where

00:03:43.860 --> 00:03:47.930
the government would put into place a set
of conditions, but the other players would

00:03:47.930 --> 00:03:49.799
make their own decisions.

00:03:49.799 --> 00:03:54.120
This explains why in Germany, trade unions,
regional governments and, of course, companies,

00:03:54.120 --> 00:03:58.500
are so important.

00:03:58.500 --> 00:04:01.370
And now you might be wondering…

00:04:01.370 --> 00:04:04.670
What are the keys for that system to work?

00:04:04.670 --> 00:04:06.650
And has it really worked out?

00:04:06.650 --> 00:04:09.439
Well let’s get right into that.

00:04:09.439 --> 00:04:11.690
AN STABLE CURRENCY

00:04:11.690 --> 00:04:16.019
I’m pretty sure you’ve heard at some point
some economist saying that devaluing a currency

00:04:16.019 --> 00:04:17.570
boosts exports, right?

00:04:17.570 --> 00:04:22.090
For instance, imagine we have a shoe manufacturing
facility in Mexico and we want to sell a pair

00:04:22.090 --> 00:04:23.750
of shoes to the American market.

00:04:23.750 --> 00:04:26.850
Let’s say, the price for those shoes is
2000 Mexican Pesos.

00:04:26.850 --> 00:04:32.310
At the time we are making this video those
200 pesos equal to 110 USD.

00:04:32.310 --> 00:04:37.500
But let’s say that, all of a sudden, the
Mexican Central Bank decides to print money

00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:39.870
and, therefore, devalue its currency.

00:04:39.870 --> 00:04:42.990
From one day to the next, that pair of shoes
could be worth 90 USD.

00:04:42.990 --> 00:04:46.580
Therefore, it would be easier to sell it abroad.

00:04:46.580 --> 00:04:49.789
This is a theory supported by most governments
today.

00:04:49.789 --> 00:04:52.710
Nevertheless, the German Government did exactly
the opposite.

00:04:52.710 --> 00:04:57.340
During the second half of the 20th century
the GERMAN MARK became one of the most stable

00:04:57.340 --> 00:05:02.240
currency on the planet.

00:05:02.240 --> 00:05:04.000
And this might lead you to wonder…

00:05:04.000 --> 00:05:09.120
How is it possible that Germany could export
so much when their products are not that cheap?

00:05:09.120 --> 00:05:12.020
Well… let’s get back to the shoes example.

00:05:12.020 --> 00:05:15.360
But this time I’m going to tell you the
story of a real company.

00:05:15.360 --> 00:05:19.600
This is a company from the North of Germany
and it’s called DESMA.

00:05:19.600 --> 00:05:25.530
It is a SME with around 200 staff.

00:05:25.530 --> 00:05:30.900
They started out right after the war in 1946,
and they specialized in making machines for

00:05:30.900 --> 00:05:31.900
shoe manufacturing.

00:05:31.900 --> 00:05:36.900
In order to do this, they needed to import
lots of machines from the United States.

00:05:36.900 --> 00:05:41.610
Thanks to having a stable currency, the price
for importing those machines was relatively

00:05:41.610 --> 00:05:42.610
low.

00:05:42.610 --> 00:05:46.289
They decided to rent a facility - at the end
of the day, since there was no inflation,

00:05:46.289 --> 00:05:50.740
rents would not go up, and they could calculate
the future cost reliably.

00:05:50.740 --> 00:05:57.030
But not only that!

00:05:57.030 --> 00:06:01.389
Since German companies couldn’t offer lower
prices, DESMA had to innovate.

00:06:01.389 --> 00:06:06.680
In 1960, they invested in state of the art
technologies to manufacture rubber-made soles.

00:06:06.680 --> 00:06:12.790
Ten years later, DESMA was the world leader
in machines to make PVC soles.

00:06:12.790 --> 00:06:17.020
It’s likely that the sole of the shoes you
are wearing today was made with a DESMA machine.

00:06:17.020 --> 00:06:21.810
This company, with 200 staff, sells to every
country in the world...

00:06:21.810 --> 00:06:24.840
including China.

00:06:24.840 --> 00:06:32.520
And this is just one example of the thousands
of small and medium sized companies, the so-called

00:06:32.520 --> 00:06:43.560
MITTELSTAND, that made up Germany.

00:06:43.560 --> 00:06:45.440
And yes, I know, I know...

00:06:45.440 --> 00:06:51.600
In 2002, the Germans abandoned the Mark and
adopted the EURO as their currency.

00:06:51.600 --> 00:06:56.370
But think about it… while other countries
like Spain were using weaker currencies before,

00:06:56.370 --> 00:07:01.639
Germany had had a strong currency for decades.

00:07:01.639 --> 00:07:06.010
That means, that in 2002, German companies
were already more competitive than anyone

00:07:06.010 --> 00:07:07.010
else’s.

00:07:07.010 --> 00:07:10.740
But hold on a minute because there are many
other things that can explain the success

00:07:10.740 --> 00:07:16.340
of the German model, so let’s dig into those
now.

00:07:16.340 --> 00:07:18.110
THE EDUCATIONAL SYSTEM

00:07:18.110 --> 00:07:19.910
I know what you are going to say now…

00:07:19.910 --> 00:07:20.910
Come on, Simon!

00:07:20.910 --> 00:07:23.080
Germany isn’t really famous for its educational
system!

00:07:23.080 --> 00:07:26.030
Just check out the PISA report...

00:07:26.030 --> 00:07:28.050
Germany is not even in the top 10!

00:07:28.050 --> 00:07:34.180
But then again, German students can find a
job right after they finish their studies.

00:07:34.180 --> 00:07:37.800
So, why is this?

00:07:37.800 --> 00:07:42.330
Well, the the answer is in their vocational
training system.

00:07:42.330 --> 00:07:46.270
Let’s imagine that you are high school student
in Germany and you don’t want to go to university.

00:07:46.270 --> 00:07:49.220
So instead you study to become a plumber.

00:07:49.220 --> 00:07:53.949
Well… from the first moment, you are already
working for a company which has an arrangement

00:07:53.949 --> 00:07:54.949
with the school.

00:07:54.949 --> 00:07:58.789
This means, from Monday to Wednesday, you
go to a classroom and from Thursday to Friday,

00:07:58.789 --> 00:08:02.659
you go the company’s workshop.

00:08:02.659 --> 00:08:07.661
There, you would be trained by the experience
plumbers and you would even get a small salary

00:08:07.661 --> 00:08:10.370
of between 300 or 500 dollars a month.

00:08:10.370 --> 00:08:12.819
And this salary is pretty important...

00:08:12.819 --> 00:08:16.599
The simple fact that the company is paying
means the employer has to make the students

00:08:16.599 --> 00:08:17.599
work.

00:08:17.599 --> 00:08:19.550
Otherwise, the firm will be throwing money
away...

00:08:19.550 --> 00:08:28.729
And the outcome of this is that German students
know how it is to be in a real life working

00:08:28.729 --> 00:08:29.729
environment.

00:08:29.729 --> 00:08:33.180
In fact, many of them remain in the company
where they did their training.

00:08:33.180 --> 00:08:37.200
And, of course, those German firms, in exchange,
get well trained workers.

00:08:37.200 --> 00:08:41.450
And now that we are talking about workers,
let’s talk about another key piece on the

00:08:41.450 --> 00:08:45.350
German system...

00:08:45.350 --> 00:08:46.690
GERMAN TRADE UNIONS

00:08:46.690 --> 00:08:51.390
In other countries like France or Spain, most
of the labour laws are made by parliament.

00:08:51.390 --> 00:08:52.390
But not so in Germany.

00:08:52.390 --> 00:08:55.780
Until 2015, Germans didn’t even have a minimum
wage...

00:08:55.780 --> 00:08:58.450
Salaries were negotiated by trade unions and
employers.

00:08:58.450 --> 00:09:00.730
In Germany, syndicates are extremely important.

00:09:00.730 --> 00:09:05.560
Actually, those big companies like AUDI or
BMW, with more than 2000 workers, are required

00:09:05.560 --> 00:09:09.560
to have union representatives on their administrative
board.

00:09:09.560 --> 00:09:15.370
Consider this: Siemens has 10 shareholder
representatives on their board and, therefore,

00:09:15.370 --> 00:09:18.350
they must have 10 people representing the
workers!

00:09:18.350 --> 00:09:20.580
Remember this one because it makes a big difference!

00:09:20.580 --> 00:09:26.160
In Spain, for instance, a big company like
TELEFONICA has no trade union representatives

00:09:26.160 --> 00:09:28.000
on their board of administration.

00:09:28.000 --> 00:09:32.860
Trade unions can only participate in another
body called the “company council”.

00:09:32.860 --> 00:09:36.510
And they can only speak about matters that
affect salaries or working conditions.

00:09:36.510 --> 00:09:41.850
By contrast, in Germany, trade unions have
a voice on every decision the company makes.

00:09:41.850 --> 00:09:45.761
When SIEMENS wants to start operating in a
new market, or launch a new product, they

00:09:45.761 --> 00:09:48.500
have to ask the shareholders…

00:09:48.500 --> 00:09:52.640
and the trade unions.

00:09:52.640 --> 00:09:56.250
And now you might be wondering…

00:09:56.250 --> 00:09:58.310
Is this just bad news for the companies?

00:09:58.310 --> 00:10:03.040
We pretty much take for granted that unions
are the enemy of a company, right?

00:10:03.040 --> 00:10:05.030
Well, it doesn’t have to be that way...

00:10:05.030 --> 00:10:06.030
Think about it.

00:10:06.030 --> 00:10:12.860
Thanks to this set up, SIEMENS and BMW workers
know whether if the company is making or losing

00:10:12.860 --> 00:10:13.860
money.

00:10:13.860 --> 00:10:16.600
And, of course, when things are going bad
for the company, they can make compromises,

00:10:16.600 --> 00:10:20.720
like reducing their working hours instead
of being fired.

00:10:20.720 --> 00:10:25.340
“Opel to cut working hours at German facilities”

00:10:25.340 --> 00:10:30.020
This explains why during the financial crisis
of 2008, German companies didn’t fire a

00:10:30.020 --> 00:10:31.700
large number of people.

00:10:31.700 --> 00:10:37.560
And this is good for the workers and the company.

00:10:37.560 --> 00:10:40.180
But wait a minute because I know what you
are thinking!

00:10:40.180 --> 00:10:44.540
Those German trade unions are collaborating
with big capitalists in order to exploit the

00:10:44.540 --> 00:10:45.540
workers!

00:10:45.540 --> 00:10:46.540
Right?

00:10:46.540 --> 00:10:47.540
Right?

00:10:47.540 --> 00:10:49.600
Well… the answer there is certainly NO.

00:10:49.600 --> 00:10:54.800
The reality is that average wage in Germany
is around $3,400 dollars a month.

00:10:54.800 --> 00:10:56.580
That’s higher than the state of New York!

00:10:56.580 --> 00:10:58.920
And that’s in a country with a pretty affordable
cost of living.

00:10:58.920 --> 00:11:02.620
A flat in Berlin is half the price of a flat
in New York City.

00:11:02.620 --> 00:11:06.930
And despite all of this Germany maintains
its position as the third biggest country

00:11:06.930 --> 00:11:08.040
when it comes to exports.

00:11:08.040 --> 00:11:11.950
So now the question is…

00:11:11.950 --> 00:11:14.690
Do you believe your country could benefit
from the German model?

00:11:14.690 --> 00:11:17.770
Or perhaps you think these policies are impossible
to implement elsewhere?

